  "completion": "## Common Mistakes with `CompletableFuture` in Java  \n### Why They Happen  \n`CompletableFuture` is a powerful, but surprisingly subtle, abstraction. The most common errors arise from:\n\n| Mistake | Why It Breaks Things | Typical Symptoms | How to Avoid |\n|---------|----------------------|------------------|--------------|\n| **Blocking the async thread** (`get()`, `join()`, `getNow()` on a running future) | The thread that would otherwise hand off the next stage is blocked, starving the pool. | Deadlocks, huge latency spikes, “pool exhausted” logs. | Use non‑blocking callbacks (`thenApply`, `thenCompose`, `whenComplete`). If you *must* block, use a dedicated single‑thread executor or the `OrTimeout` pattern. |\n| **Misusing the default executor** (`ForkJoinPool.commonPool`) | The common pool is tuned for CPU‑bound tasks; blocking I/O or long CPU bursts exhaust it. | `ForkJoinPool` “pool size 12” logs, “out of memory” due to thread growth. | Create a custom `ExecutorService` (e.g. `Executors.newCachedThreadPool()`, `Executors.newFixedThreadPool(n)`), and pass it to the async stage (`supplyAsync(..., executor)`). |\n| **Ignoring exception propagation** (`thenApply` after a failed stage) | Exceptions are wrapped in `CompletionException`; the subsequent stage will *not* run unless you handle it. | Silent failures, `NoSuchElementException` in logs. | Chain `exceptionally`, `handle`, or `whenComplete`. Example:\n\n```java\nfuture\n    .thenApply(data -> process(data))\n    .exceptionally(ex -> fallback(ex))\n    .whenComplete((res, ex) -> log(res, ex));\n```\n|\n| **Using `anyOf`/`allOf` incorrectly** | `anyOf` returns a `CompletableFuture<Object>`; you must cast to the expected type and then propagate the original futures. | `ClassCastException`, lost results. | Keep the original futures, e.g.:\n\n```java\nCompletableFuture<String> f1 = ...\nCompletableFuture<String> f2 = ...\nCompletableFuture<Void> all = CompletableFuture.allOf(f1, f2);\nString r1 = f1.join();  // after all.join()\nString r2 = f2.join();\n```\n|\n| **Wrong ordering of async stages** | Mixing `thenApply` (sync) with `thenApplyAsync` (async) on the same executor can lead to back‑to‑back serialization. | Performance regression. | Stick to one pattern per pipeline. Prefer `thenComposeAsync` when the next stage is itself async. |\n| **Unnecessary nesting** (`thenCompose` returning a future that is *already* completed) | Creates an extra future layer, consuming memory and CPU. | Slight overhead, harder to read. | Return the future directly if it’s already async; otherwise use `thenApply`. |\n| **Calling `join()` inside a callback** | Blocks the thread that *caused* the callback, potentially deadlocking if that thread belongs to the same pool. | Deadlocks, “cannot invoke join on the same thread” logs. | Use `thenCompose` or `whenCompleteAsync` to flatten. |\n| **Using `completeExceptionally` without canceling** | The future stays in the “completed exceptionally” state but any dependent stages that expect normal completion won’t run. | Subsequent stages are skipped, `CompletionException` never logged. | If you want to cancel downstream work, call `cancel(true)` in addition to completing exceptionally. |\n| **Mutable shared state without synchronization** | Parallel stages may race and corrupt data. | Hard‑to‑reproduce bugs, wrong results. | Prefer immutable objects, `AtomicReference`, or `synchronized` blocks. |\n| **Not shutting down executors** | The JVM keeps running because threads are still alive. | Tests hanging, production services stuck in `init`. | Call `executor.shutdown()` (or `shutdownNow()`) and await termination in tests. |\n| **Using `getNow()` as a “non‑blocking get”** | It returns the *default* value when not ready, which may hide logic errors. | Silent mis‑behaviour, data loss. | Prefer `join()` with `orTimeout` or explicit `isDone()` checks. |\n| **Over‑using `whenComplete` for side‑effects** | The method doesn’t propagate exceptions; you might swallow failures. | Bugs go unnoticed. | Use `whenComplete` for logging only; propagate with `handle` or `exceptionally`. |\n| **Ignoring `InterruptedException`** | `get()` and `join()` can throw it. | Thread termination failures, “interrupted” errors in logs. | Either propagate or convert to `CancellationException` by `future.cancel(true)` or re‑throw as a runtime exception. |\n| **Not converting to `CompletionStage`** | Mixing `CompletableFuture` and generic `CompletionStage` can lose type safety. | Compiler warnings, accidental cast errors. | Stick to `CompletionStage` for API boundaries, or expose `CompletableFuture` only internally. |\n\n---\n\n## Practical “Avoid It” Guidelines\n\n| Area | Recommendation |\n|------|----------------|\n| **API Usage** | - Use the *builder* style (`thenCompose`, `thenApply`, `thenAccept`, `whenComplete`, `exceptionally`, `handle`) rather than the old `runAsync`, `supplyAsync` pattern. <br>- Prefer `thenComposeAsync` over `thenCompose` if the next stage is heavy. |\n| **Thread Pool** | - Create a dedicated `ExecutorService` for your async work. <br>- Use `Executors.newCachedThreadPool()` for I/O, `Executors.newFixedThreadPool(n)` for CPU, or `ForkJoinPool.commonPool()` only for short CPU tasks. <br>- If you need to run blocking code, wrap it in `CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(..., executor)`. |\n| **Exception Handling** | - Chain `exceptionally` **after** all `thenApply`/`thenCompose`. <br>- Use `handle` if you want to both transform the result *or* recover. <br>- Log via `whenComplete` but don’t swallow exceptions. |\n| **Result Retrieval** | - Prefer `join()` *outside* of async callbacks. <br>- Use `orTimeout`/`completeOnTimeout` instead of `get(long, TimeUnit)` if you want a hard timeout. |\n| **Composition** | - For “do something, then return the same value”, use `thenApply(x -> { … ; return x; })`. <br>- For “do something and produce a new future”, use `thenCompose`. <br>- For “do something after completion, ignore the value”, use `thenRun`. |\n| **Testing** | - Use `CompletableFuture.get(long, TimeUnit)` with a generous timeout in tests. <br>- If using mocks, make sure the mocked async method returns a *completed* future. <br>- Verify that `isDone()` is true after the expected operations. |\n| **Resource Management** | - Call `executor.shutdown()` in `@AfterEach` or `@AfterAll` for unit tests. <br>- Use try‑with‑resources for `ExecutorService` wrappers (e.g., `try (var exec = Executors.newSingleThreadExecutor())`). |\n| **Avoiding Deadlocks** | - Never block on a future that might be waiting for the current thread to finish. <br>- If you need to combine multiple futures, prefer `allOf`/`anyOf` and then retrieve individual results. |\n| **Performance** | - Batch small tasks; a single `CompletableFuture` can be reused for similar work to reduce object churn. <br>- Avoid creating a new `ExecutorService` per request; keep a pool. |\n| **Code Readability** | - Keep pipelines short: chain 3–4 stages, then split into named helper methods. <br>- Use descriptive method names (`fetchUser`, `mapToDTO`, `persist`) rather than inline lambdas. |\n\n---\n\n## “What‑if” Quick Fixes\n\n| Problem | Quick Fix |\n|---------|-----------|\n| `future.join()` inside a `thenAccept` causing deadlock | Replace with `thenCompose(v -> someOtherFuture)` or use `thenAcceptAsync`. |\n| All tasks run on the same thread, causing serialisation | Add `.asyncExecutor(executor)` to each stage. |\n| `anyOf` used to get first successful result | After `anyOf`, cast the result to `String` *and* keep the original future. |\n| `getNow` returning `null` when result is not ready | Use `future.whenComplete((res, ex) -> {...})` or `future.orTimeout(2, SECONDS).join()`. |\n| Executor is never shut down | Add `executor.shutdown(); executor.awaitTermination(5, TimeUnit.SECONDS);` in a shutdown hook or test teardown. |\n| Exceptions swallowed by `whenComplete` | Switch to `handle` or add `future.exceptionally(ex -> { log(ex); return fallback; })`. |\n\n---\n\n## Sample “Well‑Behaved” Pipeline\n\n```java\nExecutorService dbPool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(10);\n\nCompletableFuture<User> userFuture = CompletableFuture\n    .supplyAsync(() -> db.fetchUser(id), dbPool)            // 1. DB call\n    .thenApplyAsync(User::normalize, dbPool)                // 2. CPU work\n    .thenComposeAsync(user -> sendWelcomeEmail(user), dbPool) // 3. Async side‑effect\n    .handleAsync((user, ex) -> {                            // 4. Exception handling\n        if (ex != null) {\n            log.error(\"Failed for id {}\", id, ex);\n            return null; // or a default User\n        }\n        return user;\n    }, dbPool);\n\nuserFuture\n    .whenComplete((res, ex) -> { if (ex != null) log.warn(\"Pipeline failed\", ex); })\n    .join(); // only at the very end, e.g. in a test or a top‑level request handler\n\n// ... at shutdown:\ndbPool.shutdown();\n```\n\n*Key take‑aways*  \n- All stages share the same dedicated executor (`dbPool`).  \n- Each stage is *async* to avoid blocking the pool thread.  \n- Exceptions are caught and logged in a single place (`handle`).  \n- `join()` is called **outside** the pipeline.\n\n---\n\n## Bottom‑Line Checklist\n\n- [ ] **Never block** inside a `CompletableFuture` callback.  \n- [ ] **Use a dedicated executor** for blocking or long‑running work.  \n- [ ] **Propagate exceptions** with `handle`/`exceptionally`.  \n- [ ] **Close executors** (`shutdown`) in production shutdown hooks or test tear‑downs.  \n- [ ] **Avoid mutable shared state**; favour immutability or proper synchronization.  \n- [ ] **Read the Javadoc** for `CompletableFuture`—the API deliberately encourages a *non‑blocking* mindset.  \n\nFollow these guidelines and you’ll avoid the most common pitfalls while enjoying the full power of Java’s asynchronous programming model. Happy coding!",
